        { "host": "A", "text": "He said the companies appear to be listening, pointing to OpenAI shelving a new model after internal tests raised questions about whether it would follow user instructions. The labs, he said, have been very cooperative." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Benzinga reports no new proposal, no legislation and no timeline, and it's the only outlet we could open on this, so a single source. The Axios interview page refused our fetcher." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "transition",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "A", "text": "To health, and a professional body setting out what it expects of these tools in children's care." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Health, science & medicine",
      "headline": "American Academy of Pediatrics publishes a policy statement on generative AI in pediatric clinical care",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "B", "text": "The American Academy of Pediatrics published a policy statement on generative AI in paediatric clinical care. Its core position is that these tools need to be specifically designed for paediatric care, rather than adapted from adult models." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "The Academy says most generative AI tools are trained on data where children are underrepresented, and few are rigorously evaluated in paediatrics. It asks developers to incorporate paediatric datasets, address bias, and put in place privacy and security safeguards that recognise children's developmental differences." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "The lead author, Srinivasan Suresh, says there will be a need for rigorous human oversight and accountability. His co-author, R. Brandon Hunter, puts it this way: adoption is often moving faster than the evidence on how to use these tools effectively is being produced." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "It covers clinicians using AI in care, not children and families using it directly, and separate guidance for that is in development. One caveat: the journal page refused our fetcher, so everything here comes from the Academy's own release as carried by Mirage News, which makes it a single source." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "transition",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "B", "text": "Now to policy, and the appointment that leads the edition." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Policy, regulation & law",
      "headline": "Trump names DNI Jay Clayton AI czar to chair a \"Super Intelligence Force\" reporting in 120 days",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "A", "text": "Reuters, relaying a Wall Street Journal interview, reports that President Trump appointed Director of National Intelligence Jay Clayton as the administration's AI czar, leading a new White House panel tasked with reporting within 120 days on the risks and opportunities posed by AI." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Clayton told the Journal: the president asked that a group be put together, which the administration refers to as the Super Intelligence Force, to keep the US a leader in advanced AI while protecting Americans' interests. He added that the risk of not being first is high." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "The charter, as Reuters describes it, has the panel reviewing AI-related risks and the government's current reporting mechanisms for breaches, hacks and other incidents, and recommending ways to strengthen federal-response capabilities under existing authorities." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Who's on it?" },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Vice chairs Emil Michael, Scott Kupor and FTC Chair Andrew Ferguson. Members include Vice President JD Vance, Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth, Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent and Chief of Staff Susie Wiles. External advisers include David Sacks and Condoleezza Rice." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "This is an update. Yesterday we carried NBC's report that Clayton was expected to be named; what's new is the confirmation, the charter, the deadline and the membership. The Journal piece is paywalled and refused our fetcher, so this is Reuters and CNBC relaying it, and the two disagree on when Clayton became intelligence chief. We couldn't resolve that." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Policy, regulation & law",
      "headline": "White House says 17 countries endorsed a \"Kyoto Vision\" pledging expanded researcher access to AI for science",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "A", "text": "There's also an international statement carrying the same new vocabulary. The White House says 17 countries endorsed what it calls the Kyoto Vision, with ministers pledging to expand researcher access to, in the document's words, SI for science tools, scientific data, computing infrastructure, and experimental facilities." },
